Write a C++ function that takes a reference to a std::string containing markdown formatted text and returns a std::string containing html formatted text.

Response:

|-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\| Certainly! Below is a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text. This function will use a simple markdown-to-HTML conversion, which is not as robust as a full markdown parser but should suffice for basic cases.

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <iostream>

// A simple markdown-to-HTML converter
std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Simple conversion rules (this is a very basic example)
    std::string html = markdown;
    html = html.replace("<", "&lt;"); // Escape '<'
    html = html.replace(">", "&gt;");  // Escape '>'
    html = html.replace("*", "&ast;"); // Replace asterisks with HTML entities
    html = html.replace("_", "&lowbar;"); // Replace underscores with HTML entities
    html = html.replace("`", "&lowbar;"); // Replace backticks with HTML entities
    html = html.replace("~~", "<del>"); // Replace double strikethrough with HTML del tag
    html = html.replace("~~~", "<ins>"); // Replace triple strikethrough with HTML ins tag

    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "This is a **test** with *markdown* and ~~strikethrough~~.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << "Markdown: " << markdown << std::endl;
    std::cout << "HTML: " << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses simple string replacements to convert markdown syntax to HTML. For a more robust solution, you would need to use a full markdown parser library such as `markdown-it` or `nlohmann/json` with a markdown parser. Here's an example using `markdown-it`:

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <markdown_it.hpp>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Initialize markdown-it
    auto md = markdown_it::MarkdownIt();
    md.setMode(markdown_it::Mode::HTML);
    md.setSafeMode(true);

    // Convert markdown to HTML
    std::string html = md.parse(markdown);

    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "This is a **test** with *markdown* and ~~strikethrough~~.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << "Markdown: " << markdown << std::endl;
    std::cout << "HTML: " << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

In this example, we use the `markdown-it` library to handle the markdown-to-HTML conversion. You can install `markdown-it` via a package manager like `pip` or `npm` if you are using Python or Node.js, respectively. For C++, you might need to include the library in your project or use a precompiled version.

